 woman has  vice-president until the Constitution is  amended the same Congress that voted to  ratify the 14th amendment also voted to  segregate the District of Columbia  Public Schools does that then mean that  Brown versus Board of Education was  wrongly decided any method of  interpretation that makes Brown wrongly  decided is obviously one that has to be  rejected can you let's go back to one  example you gave which is that of cruel  and unusual punishment a laypersons  interpretation of unusual would sound  contextual meaning unusual relative to  something some standards some prevalent  form of punishment that is what is  typically done is that lay understanding  of what unusual means relevant to our  understanding of constitutional  originalism or not or do we need to  apply a different definition of what  unusual would mean it's relevant but not  helpful it is the first case I ever  argued in the Supreme Court was Lockyer  versus Andrade my client Leandra Andrade  was sentenced to life in prison with no  possibility of parole for 50 years for  slaying $153 with the video tips from  Kmart stores in San Bernardino  California you see the sentence even  though he never committed a violent  crime  he received the sentence even though no  one in the history of the United States  had ever received a life sentence for  shoplifting until California's three  strikes law I would think that makes it  unusual I think it's cruel the Supreme  Court five-to-four  rejected that argument and upheld the  sentence the five conservative just then  being on the court come comprising the  majority I'm curious when it comes to  your view as to the double standard that  I see often may that exists with  so-called constitutional originalist  I'll give you one example and then I'll  let you tell me whether this examples  relevant or not or maybe there are  others if one believes in a strict  originalist interpretation of the  Constitution wouldn't that follow that  with as one example with the Second  Amendment by definition any lacks in  her predation in terms of what types of  firearms that exist today that didn't in  the 18th century would be interpret  interpretations beyond what was possible  when it was originally written is that a  fair critique or is there something  missing I think it's fair but I'd even  go to a prior question which was the  meaning of the Second Amendment it says  a well-regulated militia being necessary  to the security of a free State the  right of the people to keep their arms  shall not be infringed it's clear what  the purpose was of the Second Amendment  protecting a right to have guns for the  purpose of militia service and yet it's  the conservative originalist justices  like Antonin Scalia who wanted to say oh  we'll ignore the first half of the  Second Amendment it's only the latter  language both right to keep the people  people to keep and bear arms that's  relevant but if we were gonna protect  arms does that then just mean the kind  of weapons that existed in 1791 when we  talk about freedom of the press does  that apply to the new media that's  developed since 1791 obviously for the  First Amendment of meaning it has to  apply to all of the media it wouldn't  make sense if there's a second right to  limit it to the weapons that existed in  1791 hmm I've pointed out that with with  many political arguments like for  example taxes or abortion there's a kind  of systemic messaging advantage that the  right has and what I mean by that is as  an example with taxes hey it's your  money you should get to keep it the  lower the taxes the better off we are  that's very concise pithy and appealing  in many ways the left on the other hand  is not advocating the opposite which is  the more taxes the better it's well we  need to find the right balance of what's  fair but what properly funds the  institutions of government that so we're  already kind of in a more complex  position when it comes to this issue of  constitutional originalism does the  right have the same advantage in the  sense that they say hey interpret it as  written period and the left's position  is not the opposite it's not whatever  you think it means is what it means it's  you're already  sort of at a structural disadvantage  well the right has a simplistic slogan  judges should apply the law not make the  law justices should be umpires we should  just follow the framers intent but of  course all of that is a big lie everyone  knows that justices and judges make the  law as well as applier Supreme Court  justices aren't umpires they're the ones  who make the rules not just the ones who  apply it there have to balance competing  interests all the time whereas baseball  umpires don't have to do that an  originalist conservatives don't follow  originalism when it doesn't serve their  purpose yet liberals don't have a pithy  easy way to describe their approach to  constitutional interpretation yes so so  what's the what's the answer in other  words how would you advise the left to  discuss this issue first show that the  right is an emperor with no clothes that  they're not doing what they say they're  doing and second that it would be very  undesirable to do what they say they're  doing what they would be saying is make  Brown versus Board of Education  unconstitutional they make it  unconstitutional if the woman is  president or vice president so I think  we need to show the emptiness of what  they've posed and ultimately throughout  American history very few Supreme Court  justice has been originalist where the  Supreme Court has done for over 200  years is look at the text look at the  intent look at tradition look at  precedent look at contemporary policy  needs and use those to decide the  meaning of the Constitution it's always  been that right the leftist has to  defend that's the way it should be so  let's talk about another aspect that  that the left I think should be  defending when it comes to the